From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Tiberiu Chibici <chibicitiberiu@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 1 Nov 2026 16:21:02 +0200
Subject: Swap back a CD rip whose samples come out byte-swapped

On a PlayStation 3 (ppc64, big-endian) cdparanoia 3.10.2+debian-14+b1
writes its WAV with every sample byte-swapped, and quake2-music came out
as white noise. A 5 s rip of Quake II's track 2 had a zero-crossing
rate of 0.50 as stored and 0.13 read the other way round, and the
swapped bytes are the CD image's own (at the drive's 708-byte read
offset). cdparanoia's -E and -L (the drive's byte order) gave identical
output, -p (host order) the same bytes as -r (little-endian), and only
-R (big-endian) came out little-endian.

After each rip, game_data_packager/cd_byte_order.py reads 5 s from the
middle of the WAV's data chunk and compares the zero-crossing rate of
the first channel as stored with the byte-swapped one. The data chunk is
swapped in place, with one warning line, only when the stored rate is
above 0.35 and the swapped one under half of it: music is about 0.1,
swapped music about 0.5, and a genuinely noisy track is high both ways
and left alone. Rips that are right are not touched, on any host.

Forwarded: not yet (the cdparanoia bug is the real fix)

